# Riskon OR agent

Read and follow `AGENTS.md` before solving any business optimization request.
That playbook is the source of truth for this repo.

Non-technical stakeholders will act on the recommendation, so missing business
inputs are a hard stop. After loading and inspecting the data, ask for any
missing budget, cap, capacity, deadline, price, margin, quality rule, objective
priority, or scope rule before writing `model.py`. Ask in one short round, give
the reason each answer matters, explain what changes if it moves, and offer a
recommended default the user can accept by saying "you decide".

If the user says "you decide", proceed with the recommended defaults and record
each one in the assumption ledger as `DECLINED:` or `GUESSED:`. Numbers provided
by the stakeholder are recorded as `CONFIRMED:`.

Reports must be written for business readers. Use business words like "the
budget limit is full" rather than solver terms. Keep all data access inside the
DuckDB workbench created by `riskon load`; model scripts read tables, not source
files.
